<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/davidamaro/GroupFunctions.jl"><monospace>GroupFunctions.jl</monospace></ext-link><xref ref-type="fn" rid="fn1">1</xref>
  is a Julia library that computes individual matrix entries of
  polynomial irreducible representations of the unitary
  group <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{U}(d)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">U</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  A representation assigns a matrix to each group element and preserves
  multiplication. An irreducible representation has no nonzero proper
  subspace invariant under all its matrices. In a fixed basis, each
  matrix entry, viewed as a scalar function of the group element, is
  called a group function or <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[D]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>D</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>-function.
  For each requested group function, the user can choose either a
  symbolic expression or a numerical value at a specified input matrix.
  For <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{SU}(2)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">S</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">U</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  they reduce to the Wigner <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[D]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>D</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>-functions.</p>
  <p>The basis vectors are labelled by Gelfand-Tsetlin patterns
  (<xref alt="Gelfand &amp; Tsetlin, 1950" rid="ref-GelfandTsetlin1950" ref-type="bibr">Gelfand
  &amp; Tsetlin, 1950</xref>). The library also computes entire
  representation matrices, constructs input unitaries from
  parameterisations common in quantum optics, translates Gelfand-Tsetlin
  patterns into occupation-number kets, and computes the associated
  Schur functions.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of need</title>
  <p>The library serves researchers in mathematics and quantum physics
  who need symbolic expressions or numerical values for representation
  matrix entries. For <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[U\in\mathrm{U}(d)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>U</mml:mi><mml:mo>∈</mml:mo><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">U</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  let <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[D^{(\lambda)}(U)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msup><mml:mi>D</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>U</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  denote the matrix representing <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[U]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>U</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  in a polynomial irreducible representation labelled
  by <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\lambda]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  The entry with row label <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{out}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">o</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">u</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">t</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  and column label <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{init}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">i</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">n</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">i</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">t</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  has equivalent expressions in inner-product and bra-ket notation:</p>
  <p><disp-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[ D^{(\lambda)}_{\mathrm{out},\mathrm{init}}(U) = (D^{(\lambda)}(U) \mathrm{init},\mathrm{out}) =\langle \mathrm{out} \mid D^{(\lambda)}(U) \mid \mathrm{init} \rangle ,]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="block" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>D</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">o</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">u</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">t</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">i</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">n</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">i</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">t</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:msubsup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>U</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>D</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>U</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">i</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">n</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">i</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">t</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">o</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">u</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">t</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">⟨</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">o</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">u</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">t</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mo>∣</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>D</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>U</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>∣</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">i</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">n</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">i</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">t</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">⟩</mml:mo><mml:mo>,</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula></p>
  <p>where <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{init}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">i</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">n</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">i</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">t</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  and <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{out}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">o</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">u</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">t</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  are vectors in the chosen orthonormal basis, and the inner
  product <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[(\cdot,\cdot)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>⋅</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>⋅</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  is linear in its first argument.</p>
  <p>In mathematics, summing the diagonal group functions gives the
  character of the representation, namely the trace of the
  representation matrix of <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[U]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>U</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  This character is the Schur polynomial <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[s_\lambda]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>s</mml:mi><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  evaluated at the eigenvalues of <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[U]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>U</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  a central object in algebraic combinatorics and symmetric function
  theory.</p>
  <p>In quantum optics, group functions give photon transition
  amplitudes through linear optical networks
  (<xref alt="de Guise et al., 2014" rid="ref-deGuise2014" ref-type="bibr">de
  Guise et al., 2014</xref>). They also characterise the performance of
  quantum devices
  (<xref alt="Amaro-Alcalá, 2026" rid="ref-amaroalcala2025" ref-type="bibr">Amaro-Alcalá,
  2026</xref>) and describe the symmetry properties of states
  (<xref alt="Otto &amp; Szymański, 2024" rid="ref-OttoSzymanski2024" ref-type="bibr">Otto
  &amp; Szymański, 2024</xref>). In <sc>BosonSampling</sc>
  (<xref alt="Aaronson &amp; Arkhipov, 2011" rid="ref-Aaronson2011" ref-type="bibr">Aaronson
  &amp; Arkhipov, 2011</xref>), the transition amplitude is proportional
  to a permanent whose evaluation is classically computationally hard.
  Whether boson samplers retain a computational advantage over classical
  sampling algorithms under realistic noise remains an open
  question.</p>
  <p>These tasks can require numerical or symbolical group functions.
  Both are supported by <monospace>GroupFunctions.jl</monospace>; the
  following comparison concerns the symbolic case. To our knowledge,
  none of the related packages discussed below is designed to compute a
  single representation-matrix entry as an exact
  symbolic <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[D]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>D</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>-function.
  We therefore compare the cost of computing one entry directly, via the
  method employed by this library (the Grabmeier-Kerber formula
  (<xref alt="Grabmeier &amp; Kerber, 1987" rid="ref-Grabmeier1987" ref-type="bibr">Grabmeier
  &amp; Kerber, 1987</xref>), see the
  <xref alt="Software design" rid="software-design">Software
  design</xref> section), with the conventional procedure of
  constructing the Lie-algebra representation matrices and
  exponentiating the resulting full matrix.</p>
  <p>The cost of forming the full matrix depends on the representation
  dimension <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[r_\lambda(d)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  the number of rows and columns in <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[D^{(\lambda)}(U)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msup><mml:mi>D</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>U</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  Fix a Young diagram <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\lambda]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  with <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[N=\sum_i \lambda_i>1]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>N</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mo>∑</mml:mo><mml:mi>i</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi><mml:mi>i</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo>&gt;</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  boxes and at most <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  rows. The basis vectors are indexed by semistandard Young tableaux of
  this shape with entries in <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\{1,\ldots,d\}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>…</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  The hook-content formula counts these tableaux
  (<xref alt="Fulton, 1997, sec. 4.3" rid="ref-Fulton1997" ref-type="bibr">Fulton,
  1997, sec. 4.3</xref>, p. 55). The
  dimension <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[r_\lambda(d)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  of the irreducible representation is also the Schur
  polynomial <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[s_\lambda]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>s</mml:mi><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  evaluated at <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  arguments equal to 1, because a representation’s character at the
  identity equals its dimension:</p>
  <p><disp-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[r_\lambda(d)=s_\lambda(\underbrace{1,\ldots,1}_{d})
  =\prod_{(i,j)\in\lambda}\frac{d+j-i}{h_{ij}}.]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="block" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>s</mml:mi><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:munder><mml:munder><mml:mrow><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>…</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow><mml:mo accent="true">⏟</mml:mo></mml:munder><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:munder><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:munder><mml:mo>∏</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>i</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>j</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>∈</mml:mo><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:munder><mml:mfrac><mml:mrow><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mi>j</mml:mi><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:mi>i</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>h</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>i</mml:mi><mml:mi>j</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msub></mml:mfrac><mml:mi>.</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula></p>
  <p>The box <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[(i,j)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>i</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>j</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  in row <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[i]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>i</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  and column <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[j]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>j</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  has content <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[j-i]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>j</mml:mi><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:mi>i</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  Its hook length <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[h_{ij}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>h</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>i</mml:mi><mml:mi>j</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  counts the box itself, those to its right in the same row, and those
  below it in the same column. For fixed <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\lambda]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  the product has <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[N]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  factors linear in <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  and a constant denominator, giving <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[r_\lambda(d)=\Theta(d^N)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  Here <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\Theta(d^k)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mi>k</mml:mi></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  denotes growth bounded above and below by positive constant multiples
  of <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d^k]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mi>k</mml:mi></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  as <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d\to\infty]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo>→</mml:mo><mml:mi>∞</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  For example, <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\lambda=(2)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  gives <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[r_{(2)}(d)=d(d+1)/2]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mi>/</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.</p>
  <p>To compute one group function directly, we keep the two tableau
  labels fixed as <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  grows and supply the corresponding basis vectors, avoiding enumeration
  of the full basis. Under these assumptions, the current symbolic
  implementation has time complexity <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\Theta(d^3)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  and space complexity <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\Theta(d^2)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  The cubic cost arises from constructing the tables that index the
  monomial sum in the Grabmeier-Kerber formula: recursively assembling
  each length-<inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  row copies successively longer arrays,
  requiring <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\Theta(d^2)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  operations per row across <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  rows. With <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[N]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  fixed, the number of tables and permutations remains bounded
  as <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  grows. The mathematical sum runs over
  the <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[N!]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>N</mml:mi><mml:mi>!</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  permutations of <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[S_N]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>S</mml:mi><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  grouped into double cosets. The implementation pads tableau contents
  when <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d>N]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo>&gt;</mml:mo><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  so the enumeration can include additional permutations; for
  example, <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\lambda=(2,0,0)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>0</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>0</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  yields six rather than two. This padding leaves the permutation count
  bounded for fixed <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[N]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  as <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  grows. The operation count includes table construction, monomial
  preparation, and evaluation of the final sum. Scalar arithmetic
  operations, array accesses, and copies of individual entries are each
  assigned cost <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[O(1)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>O</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  Space complexity is the maximum number of scalar entries stored
  simultaneously. Constants may depend on <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[N]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>;
  bit complexity is excluded.</p>
  <p>For comparison, consider constructing the representation matrix by
  exponentiating a Lie-algebra matrix. Assume that
  this <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[r_\lambda(d)\times r_\lambda(d)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>×</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  Lie-algebra matrix has already been constructed
  (<xref alt="Barut &amp; Rączka, 1986, p. 280" rid="ref-BarutRaczka1986" ref-type="bibr">Barut
  &amp; Rączka, 1986, p. 280</xref>). Numerical scaling and squaring
  with a Padé approximant uses dense matrix products and a linear solve
  (<xref alt="Higham, 2005" rid="ref-Higham2005" ref-type="bibr">Higham,
  2005</xref>). With the Padé degree and number of squarings fixed,
  standard dense multiplication and elimination
  require <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\Theta(r_\lambda(d)^3)=\Theta(d^{3N})]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  scalar arithmetic operations and have space
  complexity <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\Theta(r_\lambda(d)^2)=\Theta(d^{2N})]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>r</mml:mi><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  Each scalar addition, subtraction, multiplication, or division has
  unit cost; numerical implementations use fixed-precision arithmetic.
  For <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\lambda=(2)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>λ</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  these costs grow as <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d^6]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mn>6</mml:mn></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  and <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d^4]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mn>4</mml:mn></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  respectively, compared with <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d^3]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  and <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d^2]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  for the direct calculation. Exponentiation produces the full matrix at
  a chosen <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[U]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>U</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>;
  the direct calculation produces one exact polynomial in its entries.
  The comparison quantifies the cost of forming the full matrix when
  only one entry is needed, with <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[N]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>N</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  fixed. Numerical methods designed to compute individual entries are
  outside this comparison.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="state-of-the-field">
  <title>State of the field</title>
  <p>Related packages support representation theory and quantum optics,
  with different computational goals.
  <monospace>SUNRepresentations.jl</monospace>
  (<xref alt="QuantumKitHub contributors, 2020" rid="ref-SUNRepresentations" ref-type="bibr">QuantumKitHub
  contributors, 2020</xref>) and the algorithm in the appendix of Alex
  et al.
  (<xref alt="2011" rid="ref-Alex2011" ref-type="bibr">2011</xref>)
  compute <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{SU}(d)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">S</mml:mi><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">U</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  Clebsch-Gordan coefficients. <monospace>RepLAB</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Rosset et al., 2021" rid="ref-RepLAB" ref-type="bibr">Rosset
  et al., 2021</xref>) manipulates irreducible representations of
  various groups, including <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{U}(d)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">U</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  but provides only indirect numerical access to group functions.
  <monospace>IntegrateUnitary.jl</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Pawela &amp; Puchała, 2026" rid="ref-IntegrateUnitary" ref-type="bibr">Pawela
  &amp; Puchała, 2026</xref>) performs symbolic integration over compact
  groups, and <monospace>haarpy</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Cardin et al., 2026" rid="ref-cardin2024haarpy" ref-type="bibr">Cardin
  et al., 2026</xref>) implements Weingarten-calculus methods. In
  quantum optics, <monospace>BosonSampling.jl</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Seron &amp; Restivo, 2024" rid="ref-Seron2024" ref-type="bibr">Seron
  &amp; Restivo, 2024</xref>), <monospace>Perceval</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Heurtel et al., 2023" rid="ref-Heurtel2023" ref-type="bibr">Heurtel
  et al., 2023</xref>), and <monospace>QOptCraft</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Gómez Aguado et al., 2023" rid="ref-QOptCraft" ref-type="bibr">Gómez
  Aguado et al., 2023</xref>) numerically model linear optical devices,
  while <monospace>The Walrus</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Gupt et al., 2019" rid="ref-Gupt2019" ref-type="bibr">Gupt
  et al., 2019</xref>) computes amplitudes for Gaussian boson sampling.
  None of these packages targets the symbolic computation of individual
  representation-matrix entries, the primary purpose of
  <monospace>GroupFunctions.jl</monospace>.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="software-design">
  <title>Software design</title>
  <p>The library computes matrix entries of polynomial irreducible
  representations of <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{U}(d)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">U</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>d</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  specified by integer partitions of length at
  most <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[d]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>d</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  using a unified method that also supports symbolic input matrices.
  Several computational routes are possible. For symmetric irreducible
  representations, which model fully indistinguishable bosons, states
  can be manipulated as polynomials of creation operators applied to the
  vacuum. Another approach constructs and exponentiates the Lie algebra
  generators in the chosen representation. Both approaches are
  computationally expensive. More restricted methods use generating
  functions
  (<xref alt="Prakash, 1996" rid="ref-prakash1996wigner" ref-type="bibr">Prakash,
  1996</xref>).</p>
  <p>The authors chose the Grabmeier-Kerber formula
  (<xref alt="Grabmeier &amp; Kerber, 1987" rid="ref-Grabmeier1987" ref-type="bibr">Grabmeier
  &amp; Kerber, 1987</xref>) for its generality. The formula expresses a
  requested matrix entry as a sum of monomials in the input matrix
  entries, with coefficients determined by the irreducible
  representation and the chosen basis vectors. Our implementation
  optimises the enumeration of the double cosets that index this sum by
  grouping permutations that contribute the same monomial. The library’s
  main function, <monospace>group_function</monospace>, evaluates the
  resulting sum.</p>
  <p>Internally, the algorithm represents basis vectors as semistandard
  Young tableaux. The public interface exposes the equivalent
  Gelfand-Tsetlin patterns through the <monospace>GTPattern</monospace>
  data structure and provides utilities such as
  <monospace>occupation_number</monospace> for common quantum optics
  calculations.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact-statement">
  <title>Research impact statement</title>
  <p><monospace>GroupFunctions.jl</monospace> has been used to evaluate
  matrix entries and group characters in published work on filtered
  randomized benchmarking
  (<xref alt="Amaro-Alcalá, 2026" rid="ref-amaroalcala2025" ref-type="bibr">Amaro-Alcalá,
  2026</xref>). Development has continued since 2020, with tests run
  through continuous integration. The package is registered in the Julia
  General registry, distributed under the MIT licence, and installed
  with the following command:</p>
  <code language="julia"> ] add GroupFunctions</code>
  <p>The following example computes a symbolic matrix entry between two
  basis vectors of a mixed-symmetry irreducible representation
  of <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{U}(4)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">U</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.</p>
  <code language="julia">λ = [2,2,1,0]; basis=basis_states(λ); # integer partition and basis
group_function(λ, basis[1], basis[end]) # symbolic matrix entry</code>
  <p>The result is a polynomial in the entries of
  the <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathrm{U}(4)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">U</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  matrix:</p>
  <preformat>u_4_1^2*u_2_3*u_3_2^2 + u_4_2^2*u_2_3*u_3_1^2 - u_4_1^2*u_2_2*u_3_2*u_3_3 
- u_4_2^2*u_2_1*u_3_1*u_3_3 - u_4_3*u_4_1*u_2_1*u_3_2^2 
- u_4_3*u_4_2*u_2_2*u_3_1^2 + u_4_1*u_4_2*u_2_1*u_3_2*u_3_3 
+ u_4_1*u_4_2*u_2_2*u_3_1*u_3_3 - 2*u_4_1*u_4_2*u_2_3*u_3_1*u_3_2 
+ u_4_3*u_4_1*u_2_2*u_3_1*u_3_2 + u_4_3*u_4_2*u_2_1*u_3_1*u_3_2</preformat>
  <p>The output printed by Julia denotes the matrix entries by
  <monospace>u_i_j</monospace>; writing the same expression using
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[u_{i,j}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>i</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>j</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  makes it easier to read:</p>
  <p><disp-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[ \begin{aligned} &u_{4,1}^{2}u_{2,3}u_{3,2}^{2}  ~+\!u_{4,2}^{2}u_{2,3}u_{3,1}^{2} ~-\!u_{4,1}^{2}u_{2,2}u_{3,2}u_{3,3} ~-\!u_{4,2}^{2}u_{2,1}u_{3,1}u_{3,3} ~-\!u_{4,3}u_{4,1}u_{2,1}u_{3,2}^{2} \\
  - &u_{4,3}u_{4,2}u_{2,2}u_{3,1}^{2} ~+\!u_{4,1}u_{4,2}u_{2,1}u_{3,2}u_{3,3}~+\!u_{4,1}u_{4,2}u_{2,2}u_{3,1}u_{3,3}~-\!2u_{4,1}u_{4,2}u_{2,3}u_{3,1}u_{3,2} \\
  + &u_{4,3}u_{4,1}u_{2,2}u_{3,1}u_{3,2} ~+\!u_{4,3}u_{4,2}u_{2,1}u_{3,1}u_{3,2}. \end{aligned}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="block" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mtable><mml:mtr><mml:mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"></mml:mtd><mml:mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msubsup><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msubsup><mml:mspace width="0.222em"></mml:mspace><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mspace width="-0.167em"></mml:mspace><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msubsup><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msubsup><mml:mspace width="0.222em"></mml:mspace><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:mspace width="-0.167em"></mml:mspace><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msubsup><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:mspace width="0.222em"></mml:mspace><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:mspace width="-0.167em"></mml:mspace><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msubsup><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:mspace width="0.222em"></mml:mspace><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:mspace width="-0.167em"></mml:mspace><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msubsup></mml:mtd></mml:mtr><mml:mtr><mml:mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"><mml:mi>−</mml:mi></mml:mtd><mml:mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msubsup><mml:mspace width="0.222em"></mml:mspace><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mspace width="-0.167em"></mml:mspace><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:mspace width="0.222em"></mml:mspace><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mspace width="-0.167em"></mml:mspace><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:mspace width="0.222em"></mml:mspace><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:mspace width="-0.167em"></mml:mspace><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub></mml:mtd></mml:mtr><mml:mtr><mml:mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"><mml:mi>+</mml:mi></mml:mtd><mml:mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:mspace width="0.222em"></mml:mspace><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mspace width="-0.167em"></mml:mspace><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>4</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:msub><mml:mi>u</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msub><mml:mi>.</mml:mi></mml:mtd></mml:mtr></mml:mtable></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula></p>
  <p>For symmetric irreducible representations, matrix entries are
  proportional to permanents, which symbolic algebra packages also
  compute. For mixed-symmetry representations, no other software
  computes these entries symbolically. Further examples, applications,
  and mathematical background are available in
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://davidamaro.github.io/GroupFunctions.jl/dev/">the
  documentation</ext-link>.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="ai-usage-disclosure">
  <title>AI usage disclosure</title>
  <p>OpenAI Codex (GPT-5.3) assisted in producing a faster version of
  the double-coset enumeration. An earlier, AI-free implementation
  remains in the repository history. The authors developed the
  mathematical design and proof of correctness of the optimised
  algorithm. Anthropic Claude (Opus 4.8) assisted with code review and
  language editing of the documentation and manuscript. The authors
  reviewed and edited all AI-assisted changes.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="acknowledgements">
  <title>Acknowledgements</title>
  <p>We thank Dr. Hubert de Guise for discussions and bibliographic
  suggestions, and Dr. Alonso Botero for suggestions on the
  presentation. Mitacs CALAREO, DeQHOST APVV-22-0570, QUAS VEGA
  2/0164/25, Postdokgrant APD0161, and the Štefan Schwarz programme
  supported this work. David Amaro-Alcalá also acknowledges indirect
  support from the Government of Alberta and NSERC during his PhD
  studies at the University of Calgary.</p>
</sec>
</body>
